    "q-46319": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46319",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192535/assets/51558d572afe9611d7bbd49e63f71691ae4b8ed386cfa49c2d38817cd8bc7e30.png)\n\nWhat's the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, given that $f(x)$ is defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have $f(x)=0.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=1.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
