    "q-46373": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46373",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192535/assets/aa047bf7762739d439b076816b9f25dde4fdd632df08a2eb8e6424803f03a91c.png)\n\nWhat's the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, given that $f(x)$ is defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-2),$ we have $f(x)=3.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,1),$ we have $f(x)=-2.$ Notice that the point $x=-2$ is included and the point $x=1$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=2.$ Notice that the point $x=1$ is included.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\,,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ -2\\,,\\, & -2\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-46373-a",
