    "q-19304": {
      "dom_id": "step-q19304",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192535/assets/1a7b132b3efcd909ec61421c956c6808cdbfd813123e2e9341fd0116d0d3deda.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have a line with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $1.$ So the graph is the line $y=2x+1$ that ends at $(1,3),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have a line with a slope of $-2$ that passes through the point $(1,0).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-0=-2(x-1)\\,⟹\\,y=2-2x\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=2-2x$ that starts at $(1,0),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-19304-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "The graph is not a function",
              "html": "The graph is not a function"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-19304-b",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -2-2x,\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}",
